        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-equus-quagga-turing-wavelength-s0-4> ;
    dct:description "The simulation script(s) models/2026-delphi10000-equus-quagga-turing-wavelength-s0.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Equus quagga specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is carried by the MorphoEval rubric assessments." ;
    dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-equus-quagga-turing-wavelength-s0.py> ;
    schema:about "Equus quagga" ;
    schema:additionalProperty "does_not_claim: Does not claim 1.883 mm was calipered on a named Equus quagga specimen in this loop.",
        "does_not_claim: Does not claim Equus scotti was the direct anagenetic ancestor, only the assigned chronoseries predecessor.",
        "does_not_claim: Does not claim a second assessor scored this pass.",
        "does_not_claim: Does not claim the broken Schnakenberg dashes are the adult stripe geometry.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Equus scotti is represented only as the equal-diffusion ablation. Its coat is not known from this run.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Growth factor 4.707 = 1.883/0.40 is reconstructed. Anisotropic inhibitor diffusion stands in for the orientation Bard described and Staddon 2024 modelled with curvature; curvature itself is not solved.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: The 1.883 mm detail length is the slot scale, obtained by pinning the fastest ky=0 mode, not a new measurement on a fetus.",
        "reported_in_source: Bard 1977: plains-zebra stripes are about 0.4 mm apart and perpendicular to the dorsal line in the third embryonic week.",
        "reported_in_source: Murray 1981: a reaction-diffusion pre-pattern for mammalian coat markings.",
        "reported_in_source: Schnakenberg 1979: the two-species kinetics used here.",
        "reported_in_source: Turing 1952: diffusion-driven instability and a critical wavenumber.",
        "transfer_boundary: The wildlife photograph is an adult flank at centimetre scale. It tests orientation and contrast, not the 1.883 mm fetal wavelength." ;
    pk:anatomicalStructure "Turing pigment stripe / ocellus wavelength in Equus quagga" ;
    pk:aristotelianEfficientCause "1. Chemical Turing / RD" ;
    pk:aristotelianMegistonGenos "I. Enaima (Vertebrata)" ;
    pk:aristotelianPoionForm "Stripes, Spots & Ocelli" ;
    pk:aristotelianPosonLaw "Turing Wavenumber k_c" ;
    pk:aristotelianSubalternClass "Mammalia (Zootoka)" ;
    pk:biologicalGenus "Equus" ;
    pk:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-equus-quagga-turing-wavelength-s0.py; output: docs/media/equus_quagga_turing_wavelength_s0_comparison.png; organism: Equus quagga; environment: n/a; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Turing 1952: diffusion-driven instability and a critical wavenumber. | Schnakenberg 1979: the two-species kinetics used here. | Murray 1981: a reaction-diffusion pre-pattern for mammalian coat markings. | Bard 1977: plains-zebra stripes are about 0.4 mm apart and perpendicular to the dorsal line in the third embryonic week.; reconstructed_in_script: The 1.883 mm detail length is the slot scale, obtained by pinning the fastest ky=0 mode, not a new measurement on a fetus. | Growth factor 4.707 = 1.883/0.40 is reconstructed. Anisotropic inhibitor diffusion stands in for the orientation Bard described and Staddon 2024 modelled with curvature; curvature itself is not solved. | Equus scotti is represented only as the equal-diffusion ablation. Its coat is not known from this run.; transfer_boundary: The wildlife photograph is an adult flank at centimetre scale. It tests orientation and contrast, not the 1.883 mm fetal wavelength.; does_not_claim: Does not claim 1.883 mm was calipered on a named Equus quagga specimen in this loop. | Does not claim Equus scotti was the direct anagenetic ancestor, only the assigned chronoseries predecessor. | Does not claim the broken Schnakenberg dashes are the adult stripe geometry. | Does not claim a second assessor scored this pass.]; phenomenon_size: 2.1 mm (0.00213 m, log10=-2.672 m, tier=6_organ_mm); species_scale_ladder (10 runs from 2.1 mm to 3.41 m): 2.1 mm:equus-quagga-bioelectric-polarity, 4.0 mm:equus-quagga-vertex-packing, 7.6 cm:equus-quagga-turing-wavelength, 13.9 cm:equus-quagga-clock-wavefront, 45.1 cm:equus-quagga-morphogen-condensation, 58.9 cm:equus-quagga-flux-canalization, 62.0 cm:equus-quagga-elastic-buckling, 77.0 cm:equus-quagga-branching-tree" ;
